+full-width meter of the op tests (`level_flanks`): both flank points, 0.9
+tool radii either side of the walk, stand in material (the stock footprint
+inside the area that the disc may sweep) that no earlier cut and no part of
+the walk's own disc more than 2.5 radii back reached. A piece with such a
+sample counts in the narrow note, which also names a merge of two rings now.
+
+**Why.** The review of #3790 found the 495 waist cuts of fig8 Outside
+silent: the old law (no cut within 1.9 radii of the centre) passed them.
+The coordinator's decision (maintainer away): every full-width cut is named
+by the meter's own law. The pins are `fig8_outside_offset_cuts_no_move_at_full_width`
+(the note names the meter's first place) and
+`fig8_inside_offset_names_each_full_width_cut`.
+
+**Dropped.** A note for each place: the note names the count and the first
+place, as before.

From 6be6b1265d40e677647c829d17a16d41c17dec02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NeoTech <andreas@roughedge.se>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 02:14:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] fix(2d): fill rings where two rings of the one ring set
 merge

Round a part, each band of the ring set is tested for stock that a
flank of one ring meets and the swath of the other leaves (a merge of
the offsets of two sides of the part). There the band gets fills: the
same distance curve at half a step, and at a quarter and three quarters
where the half leaves stock. A fill is a level curve of the distance
from the part, so it crosses no ring, and Inside and Outside walk the
same fills (ring j is level 4 j; the mirror maps l to top + 4 - l).

fig8 Outside: 495 -> 21 full-width meter points (single samples at 7
ring corners on 3 levels, named by the narrow note). No crossing from
either side.

Refs #3744
Refs #3788

